---Find additional transcoding features under Preprocessors. Enable the features at each output individually. These features are disabled by default.
---@class aws.mediaconvert.VideoPreprocessor
---@field ColorCorrector? aws.mediaconvert.ColorCorrector Use these settings to convert the color space or to modify properties such as hue and contrast for this output. For more information, see https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mediaconvert/latest/ug/converting-the-color-space.html.
---@field Deinterlacer? aws.mediaconvert.Deinterlacer Use the deinterlacer to produce smoother motion and a clearer picture. For more information, see https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mediaconvert/latest/ug/working-with-scan-type.html.
---@field DolbyVision? aws.mediaconvert.DolbyVision Enable Dolby Vision feature to produce Dolby Vision compatible video output.
---@field DurationControl? aws.mediaconvert.DurationControl Enable integer-second duration normalization. When enabled, the output duration is adjusted to land on an exact integer-second boundary. The adjustment method (trim, compress, or pad) is chosen automatically based on how far the input duration is from the nearest integer second.
---@field Hdr10Plus? aws.mediaconvert.Hdr10Plus Enable HDR10+ analysis and metadata injection. Compatible with HEVC only.
---@field ImageInserter? aws.mediaconvert.ImageInserter Enable the Image inserter feature to include a graphic overlay on your video. Enable or disable this feature for each output individually. This setting is disabled by default.
---@field NoiseReducer? aws.mediaconvert.NoiseReducer Enable the Noise reducer feature to remove noise from your video output if necessary. Enable or disable this feature for each output individually. This setting is disabled by default. When you enable Noise reducer, you must also select a value for Noise reducer filter. For AVC outputs, when you include Noise reducer, you cannot include the Bandwidth reduction filter.
---@field PartnerWatermarking? aws.mediaconvert.PartnerWatermarking If you work with a third party video watermarking partner, use the group of settings that correspond with your watermarking partner to include watermarks in your output.
---@field TimecodeBurnin? aws.mediaconvert.TimecodeBurnin Settings for burning the output timecode and specified prefix into the output.

---Details about the media file's video track.
---@class aws.mediaconvert.VideoProperties
---@field BitDepth? aws.mediaconvert.__integer The number of bits used per color component such as 8, 10, or 12 bits. Standard range (SDR) video typically uses 8-bit, while 10-bit is common for high dynamic range (HDR).
---@field BitRate? aws.mediaconvert.__long The bit rate of the video track, in bits per second.
---@field CodecMetadata? aws.mediaconvert.CodecMetadata Codec-specific parameters parsed from the video essence headers. This information provides detailed technical specifications about how the video was encoded, including profile settings, resolution details, and color space information that can help you understand the source video characteristics and make informed encoding decisions. These fields are returned for H.264 (AVC), H.265 (HEVC), and MPEG-...
---@field ColorPrimaries? aws.mediaconvert.ColorPrimaries The color space primaries of the video track, defining the red, green, and blue color coordinates used for the video. This information helps ensure accurate color reproduction during playback and transcoding.
---@field DisplayAspectRatio? aws.mediaconvert.AspectRatio An aspect ratio expressed as a fraction with numerator and denominator values, reduced to lowest terms. Used for the sample (pixel) aspect ratio and the display aspect ratio of a video track. For example, a 720x576 anamorphic track has a sample aspect ratio of 64 / 45 and a display aspect ratio of 16 / 9. A video track can declare an aspect ratio in two independent places, and MediaConvert reports...
---@field FrameRate? aws.mediaconvert.FrameRate The frame rate of the video or audio track, expressed as a fraction with numerator and denominator values.
---@field HdrMetadata? aws.mediaconvert.HdrMetadata HDR (High Dynamic Range) metadata extracted from the container, including mastering display color volume and content light level information. This metadata is present in HDR10 and similar HDR content.
---@field Height? aws.mediaconvert.__integer The height of the video track, in pixels.
---@field MatrixCoefficients? aws.mediaconvert.MatrixCoefficients The color space matrix coefficients of the video track, defining how RGB color values are converted to and from YUV color space. This affects color accuracy during encoding and decoding processes.
---@field Rotation? aws.mediaconvert.__integer The clockwise rotation angle of the video track, in degrees, as derived from container-level metadata (e.g. the MP4 tkhd transformation matrix or the Matroska ProjectionPoseRoll element). Common values are 90, 180, and 270. This field is null when no rotation metadata is present or when the rotation is 0 degrees. For MP4, non-standard transformation matrices also yield null.
---@field SampleAspectRatio? aws.mediaconvert.AspectRatio An aspect ratio expressed as a fraction with numerator and denominator values, reduced to lowest terms. Used for the sample (pixel) aspect ratio and the display aspect ratio of a video track. For example, a 720x576 anamorphic track has a sample aspect ratio of 64 / 45 and a display aspect ratio of 16 / 9. A video track can declare an aspect ratio in two independent places, and MediaConvert reports...
---@field TransferCharacteristics? aws.mediaconvert.TransferCharacteristics The color space transfer characteristics of the video track, defining the relationship between linear light values and the encoded signal values. This affects brightness and contrast reproduction.
---@field Width? aws.mediaconvert.__integer The width of the video track, in pixels.

---Input video selectors contain the video settings for the input. Each of your inputs can have up to one video selector.
